Certify a phase split independently of a floating point solver (@chemistry-phase-certificates: 0, is #chapter, #hephaestus-core "Connect-phase-predictions-to-material-evidence-and-kinetic-limits.lingua#r_0069BA0003E1560758AF284230") { r_00F44FCF73DA5C87DF329AC453
The shared `symmetric_regular_solution` program proposes binodal and spinodal locations for its declared bounded χ range. Its finite precision and fixed iteration count are not mathematical error certificates. Preserve its code and use a separate argument to test an output. A convenient certificate for rational χ>2 uses rational endpoints 0<l<u<1/2 with φ′(l)<0<φ′(u) and φ″(u)>0. Curvature decreases on the left half, so it stays positive throughout [l,u]; the preceding global proof identifies its unique root as the left binodal. Symmetry encloses the right one in [1−u,1−l].

The new program reuses the exact logarithm enclosure from `entropy_checks.py` in [Investigate entropy work bounds and changing source temperatures](../../physics/core/Investigate-entropy-work-bounds-and-changing-source-temperatures.lingua#r_0099FE8B08ED994548C5F84AD2). That helper reduces a positive rational input to powers of two and a bounded ratio, then bounds the remaining logarithm series. Its proof and existing numerical work stay in the shared lesson. Adding the rational χ(1−2x) term produces an interval for φ′ without trusting a floating-point logarithm. A proposed bracket is accepted only when these intervals prove strict opposite signs and the exact curvature test passes. Unresolved signs are rejected; a bounded program need not certify every mathematically valid case.

For χ=3, l=0.07072018 and u=0.07072019 pass. A free-energy enclosure also follows: on this interval the derivative is monotone, so the maximum magnitude of its enclosed endpoint values bounds |φ′|. If m=(l+u)/2 and M is that bound, the root value lies in φ(m)±M(u−l)/2, including the logarithm-evaluation intervals. This yields approximately −0.0583413494414404<φ(a)<−0.0583413494414396. At z=0.1, φ(z)≈−0.05508297339 is strictly higher despite positive curvature, providing a certified numerical example of the proven metastability distinction.

For total n=10 mol and z=0.35, the right-phase amount is n(z−a)/(1−2a). Its derivative with respect to a has the sign of 2z−1, so evaluating the bracket endpoints encloses the amount without guessing an error bar. The left amount lies between 6.74711217 and 6.74711222 mol; the right between 3.25288778 and 3.25288783 mol. They conserve both total amount and B. These are mathematical enclosures for the stipulated model and exact inputs, not confidence intervals for a real mixture.

Eight additional brackets centered on the unchanged solver's binary floating outputs are independently certified for selected χ from 2.0001 through 20. Converting the proposed binary value to a Fraction preserves the proposed value exactly; it does not magically make the proposal accurate. Only the sign/curvature verification provides the certificate. Close to the critical point the interval for phase amounts can become sensitive as the tie width shrinks; a small residual alone does not imply a small composition error.

**Work:** reproduce the certificate, derive the value and amount enclosures, and deliberately supply a bracket around the central stationary point. Explain its rejection and compare computation error with uncertainty in χ, temperature and composition. Reuse [Use derivative information with explicit interval and error bounds](../../mathematics/core/Use-derivative-information-with-explicit-interval-and-error-bounds.lingua#r_05FA61B1A67F2964C959D41629) and [Control omitted terms with comparisons and explicit tail bounds](../../mathematics/core/Control-omitted-terms-with-comparisons-and-explicit-tail-bounds.lingua#r_00C302EEF8B1668584FF8D01D2) for controlled bounds, and preserve the shared solver and logarithm code rather than creating duplicate foundational implementations.

